  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 Days...\n\nThe forecast for Saturday remains roughly the same. High \ntemperatures today will, once again, be roughly 10 degrees below \naverage for most locations. The main branch of the jet stream \nremains overhead today which will allow for scattered showers \nacross the CWA due to increased synoptic lift in the right-entrance\nregion of a 500mb jet streak. The highest coverage of precip will\nbe in the plains of southeast Montana. Expect the coverage of \nshowers to decrease throughout the day, as the jet streak moves\neast, leading to a mostly dry Saturday night. \n\nModels continue to trend the Sunday shortwave farther to the \nnorth. This is further solidifying that the once good \nprecipitation event, is now barely existent for for forecast \narea.  With this more northerly track of the low now, we will \nactually see a slight temperature rebound on Sunday. There is \nstill a large model spread on how much temps will rebound, but \nhighs near average are possible before another push of cool air to\nstart the work week. \n\nWith much of our area now in the \"dry slot\" of the low on Sunday,\nthere is a chance that some stronger thunderstorms may develop in\nthe eastern part of the CWA. During the afternoon and evening \nSunday, a cold front will push from west to east across the area \nacting the the primary source of lift for any convection. Due to \nthe cooler temps, modeled MU-CAPE values remain under 1000 J/Kg \nwith surface CAPE under 500 J/Kg in recent runs. This would be \nplenty sufficient though with the strong source of lift, the cold \nfront, greatly aiding CI as the it pushes through Sunday evening. \nAdditionally, shear values in SE Montana will be in the 30-60kt \nrange due to the proximity and location of the upper level low. \nWhile 60 kts may rip the storms apart, the \"lower\" values will be \nperfect to sustain any updrafts that can establish themselves. In \nterms of moisture, PWAT values are forecast to exceed 1\" in the \neastern 1/3 of the CWA ahead of the FROPA which will be more than\nsufficient for thunderstorm development. The CAMs that run out \nthat far are depicting some strong to severe storms near the Black\nHills late Sunday and just clipping SE Montana. It is well within\nthe realms of possibility that these storms start to trend \nfarther north, just as the parent low did, over the next 2 days. \nIn terms of thunderstorm hazards, while every strong to severe \nstorm is capable of strong winds, it looks like the main threat \nwould be the potential for large hail. Additionally, any storm \nthat can become more surface based could produce a brief tornado.\n\nMonday is looking increasingly windy. As the upper level low\nmigrates east Sunday night into Monday morning, the stronger mid\nto upper level winds will be on the move with it. There is,\nnaturally, still some disagreement on timing and progression of \nthis feature. Both the ECMWF and GFS show 50kt 700mb winds over \nthe western portion of the CWA by 15z Monday with a slow and \nsteady eastward progression of this maximum through the day. While\nsurface winds will be breezy through the entire region all day, \nthe strongest gusts will likely occur with the jet max overhead. \nDuring the late morning and early afternoon hours, when the \nstrongest winds aloft are westerly over the foothills, there \nappears to be a good signal for gap enhancement along the US-191 \ncorridor (Big Timber and Harlowton). Ensembles do have several \nmembers with surface winds touching 50 kts in both locations \nmidday Monday. Trends will be monitored as this appears to be the \nfirst impactful gap wind event of the fall. Not to be forgotten \nthough, the entire CWA could see gusts of 35 to 45 mph during the\nday Monday.\n\nThrough the middle of the week we will remain under the main \nbranch of the jet stream which will allow for near to just below \naverage temperatures to linger. There are repeated chances for \nisolated showers continue with these cooler temperatures due to \nthe strong jet dynamics overhead in addition to weak vort maxes\npassing through. Towards the end of next week there are some \nhints that a similar shortwave may move through the region again. \nWMR",
